
for a small raspberry pi 5 with 8gb of ram, what is the best ollama model to run a conversational chat with tool calling functionality?
edit
Thought for 29.1 seconds







Short answer:
For a Raspberry Pi 5 (8 GB RAM, CPU‑only) the sweet spot for a “chat‑with‑tool‑calling” model in Ollama is the Llama 3.1 8 B (or, if you want a slightly lighter option, the Mistral‑7B‑Instruct). Both models run comfortably in 8‑bit mode on the Pi 5, give you solid multi‑turn chat quality, and support native function‑calling (tool‑calling) in the same way the OpenAI API does.

Below you’ll find a quick‑start guide, the trade‑off matrix, and a few practical tips on how to actually wire tool calls into the conversation.

1. Which model to pick? – Trade‑off matrix
Model	Size (B)	Quantization Needed	CPU‑Inference Speed (Pi 5)	RAM Usage (8‑bit)	Function‑Calling Support	Comment
Llama 3.1 8 B	8 B	8‑bit	~5‑10 sec/turn (single‑core)	~3.5 GB	✅ (ChatCompletion with function field)	Highest‑quality chat; good for multi‑turn reasoning.
Mistral‑7B‑Instruct	7 B	8‑bit	~4‑8 sec/turn	~3.0 GB	✅	Slightly lighter, slightly faster.
Phi‑3‑Mini‑4 B	4 B	8‑bit	~2‑4 sec/turn	~2.0 GB	❌ (no built‑in function calling)	Best for ultra‑light use, but you’ll need to implement a custom prompt‑based tool‑calling workaround.
Llama 2‑7B‑Chat	7 B	8‑bit	~4‑7 sec/turn	~3.0 GB	❌ (no built‑in function calling)	Works for chat, but you’ll need a wrapper to simulate tools.
Bottom line – If you need true function‑calling (like the OpenAI functions parameter), go with Llama 3.1 8 B or Mistral‑7B‑Instruct. The former gives a touch more natural language quality, the latter gives a little more speed and lower memory.

2. Quick‑start on the Pi 5
2.1 Install Ollama
# Pull the latest installer script
curl -fsSL https://ollama.com/install.sh | sh
The installer will install ollama binary and set up the daemon. No GPU required – it will use your Pi’s CPU.

2.2 Pull the model
# 8‑bit quantized Llama 3.1 8 B
ollama pull llama3.1:8b

# or, if you prefer Mistral
ollama pull mistral:instruct
You’ll see the model size (~8 GB for the full model). The 8‑bit quantized version fits comfortably in the 8 GB RAM.

2.3 Run a simple chat with tool‑calling
Below is a minimal Python wrapper that demonstrates function‑calling using the ollama library (you can install it with pip install ollama). Replace the call_tool() function with whatever external service you need.

import ollama
import json

# define a simple "tool" – e.g. a calculator
def calculator(operation: str, a: float, b: float):
    if operation == "add":
        return a + b
    elif operation == "subtract":
        return a - b
    elif operation == "multiply":
        return a * b
    elif operation == "divide":
        return a / b
    return None

# tool metadata for Ollama function calling
function_definitions = [
    {
        "name": "calculator",
        "description": "Perform basic arithmetic",
        "parameters": {
            "type": "object",
            "properties": {
                "operation": {"type": "string", "enum": ["add", "subtract", "multiply", "divide"]},
                "a": {"type": "number"},
                "b": {"type": "number"},
            },
            "required": ["operation", "a", "b"],
        },
    },
]

def ask_llama(prompt, model="llama3.1:8b"):
    # first round – ask for tool usage
    response = ollama.chat(
        model=model,
        messages=[{"role": "user", "content": prompt}],
        # pass function definitions so the model can return a "function call"
        tools=function_definitions,
    )
    # parse
    message = response["message"]
    if message.get("function_call"):
        # the model is telling us to call a function
        name = message["function_call"]["name"]
        args = json.loads(message["function_call"]["arguments"])
        if name == "calculator":
            tool_result = calculator(**args)
            # respond back to the model with the result
            followup = ollama.chat(
                model=model,
                messages=[
                    {"role": "user", "content": prompt},
                    {"role": "assistant", "content": message["content"]},
                    {"role": "tool", "name": name, "content": str(tool_result)},
                ],
                tools=function_definitions,
            )
            return followup["message"]["content"]
    # if no function call, just return the plain content
    return message["content"]

# example usage
print(ask_llama("What is 12 times 7?"))
What’s happening?

The first chat request includes tools=function_definitions.
If the model decides to call the calculator, it returns a function call block (message["function_call"]).
Your wrapper extracts the arguments, executes the real function, and feeds the result back as a tool message (role: "tool").
The model then continues the conversation with the tool output in context.
You can expand the function_definitions list to include as many tools as you like: weather API, DB queries, image generation, etc.

3. Tips for getting the best performance
#	Tip	Why it matters
1	Use 8‑bit quantization (--quantization 8 if you’re using ollama serve).	Cuts model size from ~12 GB to ~6 GB and speeds up inference by ~2×.
2	Keep the chat history short (≤ 4–5 turns)	Each new turn adds the entire conversation to the prompt buffer, increasing latency.
3	Batch tool calls	If your tools are slow, batch them (e.g. combine several API calls into one).
4	Turn on --low-vram (if available)	The Pi 5’s memory is limited; low‑VRAM mode reduces temporary RAM usage at the cost of a tiny bit of speed.
5	Run the daemon with --cpu	Ollama’s default uses the fastest CPU backend (OpenBLAS), but you can pin it to --cpu to avoid unnecessary overhead.
6	Turn off GPU	The Pi 5 has no discrete GPU, so make sure the model isn’t trying to use one.
4. Bottom line for your Pi 5
Choice	Quick Verdict
Llama 3.1 8 B	Best quality chat + native function calling – fits in 8 GB RAM when 8‑bit quantized.
Mistral‑7B‑Instruct	Slightly lighter – faster inference and lower RAM use, still supports function calling.
Phi‑3‑Mini	Ultra‑light – great for very low latency but you’ll need to implement tool calls yourself.
If you’re just experimenting and don’t mind a few extra seconds per turn, Llama 3.1 8 B is the safest bet. If you want a quicker, “real‑time” feel, switch to Mistral‑7B‑Instruct. Either way, you’ll get a fully local, tool‑aware conversational AI that runs entirely on your Pi 5. Happy building!


