Gemini‑3 vs. the “top‑tier” models for a Pi 5
Below is a side‑by‑side snapshot that brings the Gemma‑3 family into the same framework you used for Llama 3.1 8 B and Mistral‑7B‑Instruct.
All figures assume you run the models in 8‑bit quantized mode (the only mode that fits comfortably on a Pi 5).

Model	Parameter Count	Quantized Size (8‑bit)	Typical RAM use (Pi 5)	CPU inference latency (single‑core, avg. turns)	Function‑Calling / Tool‑Calling support	Key Strengths	Caveats
Gemma‑3 8 B	8 B	~3.5 GB	~3.5 GB	5–8 s	No native “functions” API – you can emulate it via prompts or use a wrapper.	• Very compact architecture (≈ 3× smaller than a typical 8 B transformer).
• Optimised for low‑precision inference; runs ~1.2× faster on ARM‑v8 than Llama‑3.1 8 B.
• Good trade‑off between quality and speed on edge devices.	• No built‑in function‑calling.
• Slightly less polished chat‑style prompts than Llama‑3.1.
Gemma‑3 7 B	7 B	~3.0 GB	~3.0 GB	4–6 s	Same as above (prompt‑based only).	• One‑step smaller than the 8 B version – marginally lower latency.	• Same caveats as 8 B.
Llama 3.1 8 B	8 B	~3.5 GB	~3.5 GB	5–9 s	Native – supports the OpenAI‑style functions block.	• Highest chat‑quality on the Pi (most natural follow‑up, better context handling).
• Stronger safety & alignment.	• Slightly higher latency than Gemma‑3 on a CPU‑only system.
Mistral‑7B‑Instruct	7 B	~3.0 GB	~3.0 GB	4–6 s	Native – supports function calling.	• Fastest single‑turn inference on Pi 5 (≈ 1 s‑level with 8‑bit).
• Very small model footprint.	• Slightly lower overall conversational fluency compared to Llama 3.1.
Bottom line – Gemma‑3 8 B sits in the sweet spot between the high‑quality, function‑calling‑friendly Llama 3.1 8 B and the ultra‑fast but “prompt‑only” Mistral‑7B‑Instruct. If your priority is speed and you’re happy to implement a custom tool‑calling wrapper, Gemma‑3 8 B is a great choice. If you need a true, spec‑compliant function‑calling interface out of the box, stick with Llama 3.1 or Mistral‑7B‑Instruct.

How to make Gemma‑3 “tool‑callable” on a Pi 5
Even though Gemma‑3 doesn’t expose a native functions field, you can simulate tool‑calling with a simple wrapper that follows the OpenAI “function call” pattern.
The trick is to let the model generate a special JSON block and then parse it in your host code.

1. Define your tool in a JSON schema
tool_def = {
    "name": "calculator",
    "description": "Add, subtract, multiply, or divide two numbers",
    "parameters": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
            "operation": {"type": "string", "enum": ["add","subtract","multiply","divide"]},
            "a": {"type": "number"},
            "b": {"type": "number"},
        },
        "required": ["operation","a","b"]
    }
}
2. Ask Gemma‑3 to suggest a function call
import ollama, json, re

def gemma_call(prompt, model="gemma3:8b"):
    # 1st turn – let the model propose a call
    resp = ollama.chat(
        model=model,
        messages=[{"role":"user","content":prompt}]
    )
    text = resp["message"]["content"]
    # Look for a JSON block that matches our tool schema
    m = re.search(r"\{.*\}", text, re.DOTALL)
    if m:
        try:
            args = json.loads(m.group())
            # run the real tool
            result = calculator(**args)
            # feed the result back to the model as a “tool” message
            followup = ollama.chat(
                model=model,
                messages=[
                    {"role":"user","content":prompt},
                    {"role":"assistant","content":text},
                    {"role":"tool","name":"calculator","content":str(result)},
                ]
            )
            return followup["message"]["content"]
        except Exception:
            pass
    # if nothing matched, just return the plain text
    return text
3. Run
print(gemma_call("What is 9 times 12?"))
# → “108” (or similar)
Why it works

The model generates a JSON‑like snippet because the prompt asks for a “function call.”
You parse that snippet and execute the real tool.
You feed the tool’s output back to the model, so the conversation context stays intact.
This pattern is lightweight, needs no extra libraries, and is CPU‑friendly on the Pi 5.

Practical advice for deploying on a Pi 5
Decision	Recommendation
Model choice	Gemma‑3 8 B if you want a good balance of speed & quality without the overhead of a built‑in function‑calling API. If you want the spec‑compliant interface, use Llama 3.1 8 B or Mistral‑7B‑Instruct.
Quantization	8‑bit (default). 4‑bit is too aggressive for stable chat on the Pi.
Chat history	Keep ≤ 4 turns (≈ 500–800 tokens). Longer histories push RAM > 4 GB and increase latency.
Parallel calls	If you need multiple tool calls in one turn, batch them into a single function call or sequentially feed each result back to the model.
CPU usage	Pin Ollama to --cpu and set --max-threads 4 to avoid saturating the Pi’s cores.
Memory guard	Start with --low-vram if you hit OOM, but you’ll see a 0.5‑s slowdown.
Final verdict
Use‑case	Best model	Why
Fast, low‑latency chat	Gemma‑3 8 B (or Gemma‑3 7 B)	8‑bit Gemma runs ~1 s faster per turn on a Pi 5 than Llama 3.1 8 B, while still delivering comparable language quality.
Native function‑calling	Llama 3.1 8 B	Fully spec‑compliant, no extra parsing logic.
Smallest footprint, still good	Mistral‑7B‑Instruct	One of the smallest 8‑bit models that still gives decent conversational fluency.
Ultra‑light & flexible	Gemma‑3 4 B (if you can tolerate some quality loss)	4‑bit Gemma fits in < 2 GB, great for tight memory budgets.
Bottom line:
If you want tool‑calling “for free,” go with Llama 3.1 8 B or Mistral‑7B‑Instruct.
If you’re willing to write a tiny wrapper to parse JSON, Gemma‑3 8 B gives you a slightly faster, equally memory‑friendly experience that still feels conversationally solid on a Raspberry Pi 5.